One orbit covers distance \(2\pi r\), so
\[
T=\frac{2\pi r}{v}
\]
\[
T=\frac{2\pi(7.5\times10^6)}{7.3\times10^3}
=6.5\times10^3\,\mathrm{s}
\]

\[
r=\left(\frac{GMT^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^{1/3}
\]
\[
r=\left(\frac{(6.67\times10^{-11})(5.97\times10^{24})(86164)^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^{1/3}
=4.22\times10^7\,\mathrm{m}
\]
This is the distance from Earth's center, not the altitude above the surface.

Rearrange the period relation:
\[
T^2=\frac{4\pi^2r^3}{GM}
\]
\[
M=\frac{4\pi^2r^3}{GT^2}
\]
\[
M=\frac{4\pi^2(1.8\times10^8)^3}{(6.67\times10^{-11})(3.0\times10^5)^2}
=3.8\times10^{25}\,\mathrm{kg}
\]

At the surface,
\[
g_s=\frac{GM}{R^2}
\]
so
\[
GM=g_sR^2
\]
At \(r=kR\),
\[
v=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}
=\sqrt{\frac{g_sR^2}{kR}}
=\sqrt{\frac{g_sR}{k}}
\]
The period is
\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}
=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{k^3R^3}{g_sR^2}}
=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{k^3R}{g_s}}
\]
As \(k\) increases, speed falls as \(k^{-1/2}\), while period grows as \(k^{3/2}\).
